      Stainless Steel Wire Mesh in Food &amp; Beverage Processing: Grades and Regulations
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 12 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> For food and beverage contact, specify 304 stainless wire mesh as the default and 316L where the process involves chlorides—brine, dairy cleaning chemicals, coastal humid air, or CIP acids above ambient temperature—because its 2–3% molybdenum prevents pitting that 304 cannot. The mesh itself has no "FDA approval"; compliance means the material is suitable under FDA 21 CFR 178.3290 (as an indirect additive in food-processing equipment) and EU Regulation 1935/2004, supported by a mill certificate and, for EU sales, a Declaration of Compliance from the mesh maker. Choose plain weave for precise filtration, twill for fine high-flow filtration, and wire-link or wire-mesh conveyor belting for drying and cooling. Passivate after any fabrication, and control screen blinding with aperture-to-particle sizing and regular CIP.
      </p>
    </div>

