    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Wire mesh touches food and beverage product at almost every stage: screening raw grains and sugar, filtering beer and juice, dewatering vegetables, forming and frying on mesh belts, and sieving powders before packaging. Unlike structural stainless steel, mesh has enormous surface area per kilogram—and every square millimeter of it is either food-safe or a contamination and corrosion risk. That is why grade selection and documentation matter more here than in any other mesh application. Our <a href="/applications/food-processing/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">food processing applications page</a> and <a href="/applications/filtration/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">filtration applications page</a> cover the broader product range; this article focuses on the grade and compliance decisions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Grades -->
    <section id="grades" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">304 vs 316L: Which Grade for Food Contact</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Both are austenitic stainless steels accepted for food contact worldwide. The practical difference is chloride resistance. 304 (18% Cr, 8% Ni) handles neutral foods, dry powders, and washdown with mild detergents. 316L adds 2–3% molybdenum and a capped carbon content (the "L"), which dramatically slows pitting and crevice corrosion in chlorides—and chloride is everywhere in food plants: CIP caustic/chlorinated cleaners, brining and cheesemaking, tomato and pickle acids, and the humid coastal air at seafood processors.</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Process Condition</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Recommended Grade</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Dry powder sieving (flour, sugar, spices)</td>
              <td class="p-3">304</td>
              <td class="p-3">No chlorides; lowest cost</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Beer and wine filtration, brewhouse lautering</td>
              <td class="p-3">304 (316L for sour beer / acidic CIP)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Mildly acidic but low chloride; sour programs and chlorinated CIP push pitting risk</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Dairy, cheese brine, whey</td>
              <td class="p-3">316L</td>
              <td class="p-3">Chloride-rich brines and aggressive CIP chemistry pit 304 within months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Fruit and vegetable wash, dewatering belts</td>
              <td class="p-3">304 (316L coastal / chlorinated wash water)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Organic acids mild; chlorinated wash water and salt air change the answer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Seafood processing, marinades</td>
              <td class="p-3">316L</td>
              <td class="p-3">Sodium chloride at food concentration attacks 304 via pitting under deposits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">High-temperature frying / baking belts</td>
              <td class="p-3">314 or 316, heavy wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">Higher service temperature; wire-link belts need fatigue resistance</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two procurement traps to avoid. First, "food grade 201": mills sometimes substitute 201-series or low-nickel 202 wire that magnets stick to and that corrodes in wet service—always demand a mill certificate showing chemistry, and XRF-test incoming rolls. Second, "stainless" wire mesh with a plastic or epoxy coating over carbon steel: acceptable for some dry applications, but the coating chips into product and fails in CIP. For a deep dive on the metallurgy, see our <a href="/blog/304-vs-316-stainless-steel-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">304 vs 316 stainless steel mesh comparison</a> with salt-spray test data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Regulations -->
    <section id="regulations" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Food Contact Regulations: FDA and EU 1935/2004</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        There is no certificate called "FDA-approved mesh." Regulatory compliance works differently, and understanding it prevents both under-specification and paying for paperwork you do not need.</p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>US FDA:</strong> stainless steel is treated under the food-contact substance framework; mesh used in equipment is covered indirectly by 21 CFR 178.3290 (adjuvants in resinous and polymeric coatings context) and by the general GRAS status of iron-chromium-nickel alloys for food contact. In practice, US buyers demonstrate compliance with material grade certificates and equipment fabrication to 3-A Sanitary Standards where applicable.</li>
        <li><strong>EU Regulation 1935/2004:</strong> the framework law requiring that materials contacting food do not transfer constituents in quantities that endanger health or change food composition. Articles 3 and 17 require traceability. For metallic materials, Regulation (EU) 2016/1416 (amending measure on metal alloys) sets specific release limits (e.g., 0.05 mg/kg for chromium(VI), 0.01 mg/kg for nickel migration).</li>
        <li><strong>Declaration of Compliance (DoC):</strong> EU law requires the manufacturer or importer of the mesh (or the equipment builder) to issue a DoC stating the material meets 1935/2004 and identifying the substances used. Ask your mesh supplier for it—reputable Anping mills provide DoCs with mill certificates; refusal is a red flag.</li>
        <li><strong>Migration testing:</strong> for direct long-duration contact (e.g., a mesh belt carrying product for an hour), some auditors ask for migration test data per EN 1186. Standard 304/316L stainless with proper passivation passes these tests; the cost of testing one grade is roughly $1,500–3,000 if your customer demands a report.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        For most sieving, filtration, and belt applications, the procurement package that satisfies auditors is: <strong>mill certificate (EN 10204 3.1) with chemistry</strong>, <strong>Declaration of Compliance to 1935/2004</strong>, and <strong>surface finish/passivation statement</strong>. That combination covers FDA and EU expectations without custom testing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Weaves -->
    <section id="weaves" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Weave Types for Filtration, Drying, and Conveying</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Weave / Belt Type</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Range</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Food &amp; Beverage Use</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Plain weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–400 mesh (20–1500 µm)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Sifting, straining, filter leaves, screens</td>
              <td class="p-3">Precise cut point; standard for screeners</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Twill weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">250–635 mesh (20–28 µm)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Fine filtration of syrups, beer polishing</td>
              <td class="p-3">Stable at high mesh counts where plain weave fails</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Dutch weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">1–40 µm absolute</td>
              <td class="p-3">Trap filtration, yeast and haze removal</td>
              <td class="p-3">High pressure drop; clean-in-place only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Wire-link / balanced weave belt</td>
              <td class="p-3">Wire 1.0–3.0 mm, openings 4–20 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">Drying, cooling, freezing tunnels; baking; frying</td>
              <td class="p-3">Positive drive; specify 316L for washdown zones</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Flat wire belt</td>
              <td class="p-3">Flat 8 × 1 mm, openings 3–10 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">Heavy-duty conveying, draining, blanching</td>
              <td class="p-3">High open area, easy cleaning, long spans</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two selection details buyers miss. On <strong>filter screens</strong>, specify mesh count by the absolute or nominal micron cut you need and verify with a bubble-point or particle-retention test—not by "mesh count" alone, which tolerances can make ambiguous. On <strong>belts</strong>, specify the weave's edge treatment (welded U-edge vs looped) and the drive sprocket pitch, because belt life is usually limited by edge failure, not wire wear. Our <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">stainless steel wire mesh product range</a> covers woven mesh for filtration and sieving; belts are usually quoted as custom fabrications. When converting between mesh count and micron for specs, use the <a href="/tools/mesh-count-conversion/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">mesh count conversion tool</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Cleaning -->
    <section id="cleaning" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Cleaning, Passivation, and Hygienic Design</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Stainless steel resists corrosion because of a chromium-oxide film—but weaving, welding, and fabrication embed free iron and damage that film. Two practices keep mesh food-safe:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Passivation after fabrication:</strong> a citric or nitric acid bath dissolves embedded iron and restores the oxide layer. Specify "pickled and passivated" on any mesh that is cut, welded, or formed after weaving. Unpassivated welds rust within weeks in washdown service and can contaminate product with iron particles.</li>
        <li><strong>CIP compatibility:</strong> confirm the mesh tolerates your cleaning regimen—typically 1–2% caustic at 60–80°C with peracetic acid or hypochlorite stages. 316L handles chlorinated CIP at these concentrations; 304 needs low-chloride (&lt;50 ppm) final rinses.</li>
        <li><strong>Hygiene by design:</strong> choose crevice-minimizing constructions—smooth welded seams on frames, no wire ends in product zone, open constructions that drain and dry. A mesh that traps product between wires and frame becomes a microbial harbor that CIP cannot reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Drying:</strong> chlorides concentrate as rinse water evaporates. Mesh that sits wet overnight pits at contact points—design frames so belts and screens dry, or add a final fresh-water rinse.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Verification is simple: a <strong>ferroxyl (free-iron) test</strong> on welds and cut edges after passivation confirms treatment worked, and a wipe test for ATP or protein validates cleaning. Add both to commissioning and periodic QA.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Blinding -->
    <section id="blinding" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Screen Blinding and Throughput Problems</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Blinding—particles near the aperture size wedging into openings—is the dominant uptime problem on food sifters and dewatering screens, driven by three factors:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Near-size fraction:</strong> if more than about 15–20% of the feed sits within ±25% of the aperture, blinding risk is high. Remedies: open the aperture one step, use a slotted aperture, or add ultrasonic or ball-cleaning decks.</li>
        <li><strong>Moisture and fat:</strong> hygroscopic sugars, starches, and oil-coated nuts blind mesh far below expected moisture levels. Remedies: heated decks, deblinding sliders or balls under the screen, or a coarser upper scalping mesh to remove the fraction doing the damage.</li>
        <li><strong>Electrostatics:</strong> fine spice and powder sieving at low humidity suffers static binding. Remedies: grounded frames, conductive mesh grades, or ultrasonic screening at 20–40 kHz.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Cost context: a blinding-related stoppage on a packaging line costs far more per hour than the mesh. When specifying, ask the supplier for open-area percentage and recommended anti-blinding accessories for your exact product—reputable mills run sieving trials on customer samples for a modest fee, which is cheaper than discovering incompatibility in production.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Specifying -->
    <section id="specifying" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Specifying and Verifying Food-Grade Mesh</h2>

      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Food-Grade Mesh RFQ Checklist</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Grade: 304 or 316L, with EN 10204 3.1 mill certificate required</li>
            <li>☐ Weave type and mesh count / micron cut point with tolerance</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter and tolerance (±0.01 mm typical for fine mesh)</li>
            <li>☐ Surface state: pickled and passivated; free-iron test on welds</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Declaration of Compliance to EU 1935/2004 (for EU-bound equipment)</li>
            <li>☐ Cleaning regimen compatibility stated (chemistry, temperature, concentration)</li>
            <li>☐ Edge/frame finish for product-zone hygiene</li>
            <li>☐ Anti-blinding accessories or sieving trial if near-size fraction exceeds 15%</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        On arrival, verify before installation: XRF chemistry check on one roll per lot (takes minutes at any metal lab), micrometer check of wire diameter, and a visual pass for weaving defects and stray wire ends. Keep certificates with the equipment file—food-safety auditors will ask for traceability from mesh lot to installation date. If you are importing from China for the first time, our <a href="/blog/how-to-import-wire-mesh-from-china/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">wire mesh import guide</a> covers supplier verification and inspection in detail.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is stainless steel wire mesh FDA approved?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            There is no FDA "approval" for mesh as a product. In the US, stainless steel is accepted for food contact under the food-contact substance framework (21 CFR 178.3290 context and general GRAS status of iron-chromium-nickel alloys), and compliance is demonstrated through material grade documentation and hygienic fabrication. In the EU, Regulation 1935/2004 requires a Declaration of Compliance from the material supplier or equipment builder. Ask for a mill certificate (EN 10204 3.1) plus a DoC—together they satisfy auditors in both markets.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">304 or 316L—which do I need for my food process?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Default to 304 for dry powders, neutral beverages, and low-chloride washdown. Move to 316L when the process involves chlorides: dairy brines, chlorinated CIP chemistry, seafood, marinated or pickled products, sour beer programs, or coastal salt air. The molybdenum in 316L prevents pitting corrosion that quietly destroys 304 mesh at weld points and under product deposits. See the <a href="/blog/304-vs-316-stainless-steel-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">304 vs 316 comparison</a> for test data.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which weave type should I use for filtration versus drying?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For filtration and sieving: plain weave for cut points above about 40 µm, twill weave for 20–40 µm where plain weave loses stability, and dutch weave for absolute ratings under 40 µm. For drying, cooling, and thermal processing: wire-link (balanced weave) or flat-wire conveyor belts, which are open, easy to clean, and run on positive sprocket drives. Belts are custom fabrications—specify wire grade, belt pitch, and edge treatment.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Do I need to passivate stainless mesh, and how is it verified?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes, whenever the mesh is cut, welded, or formed after weaving—those operations embed free iron that rusts and can shed into product. Passivation (citric or nitric acid bath) removes the iron and restores the chromium-oxide layer. Specify "pickled and passivated" on the order and verify with a ferroxyl (free-iron) test on welds and cut edges; blue spotting indicates inadequate treatment.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">My sieve screens keep blinding—what are my options?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Blinding comes from a high near-size fraction (over 15–20% within ±25% of the aperture), moisture or fat in the product, or electrostatics. Options in order of cost: open the aperture one step, add a scalping mesh above to remove the problem fraction, use slotted apertures, add mechanical deblinding (balls or sliders), ground the frame against static, or go to ultrasonic screening for fine powders. A sieving trial with your actual product on candidate meshes settles the choice cheaply before you commit.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

